# Handle a request for human approval

Review the proposed tool name and arguments before you approve a parked session.
Check the trigger description and the time left to respond. Approve only the
operation you intend to allow. Reject an unwanted operation. If the request
allows edits, correct its arguments before you submit it.

A session is one task the agent is trying to finish. A tool call is an operation
the agent proposes, such as deleting a record. Human-in-the-loop approval pauses
that call until you resolve the request. The agent must not execute the call,
retry it, or bypass the approval while it waits.

## Choose the requested action

`action_required` tells the interface what to ask you to do.
`resolution_status` records the outcome of that request. A new request starts
with resolution `PENDING`.

| `action_required` | What you see and do | Final resolutions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `APPROVE_REJECT_MODIFY` | An approval form shows the tool and proposed arguments. Approve them, submit corrected arguments, or reject the call. The default financial trigger names `TransactionApprovalModal`. | `APPROVED`, `MODIFIED`, `DENIED` |
| `MFA_CHALLENGE` | A security panel asks you to complete multifactor authentication before approval. Reject the request if you do not authorize it. The default deletion trigger names `SecurityChallengePanel`. | `APPROVED`, `DENIED`, `TIMEOUT` |
| `NOTIFY_ONLY` | An informational notice shows the matched trigger and proposed call. No approval or correction is required. | No blocking resolution. It never releases a session from `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL`. |

`NOTIFY_ONLY` never blocks execution. It must not create an approval wait of its
own or release a wait caused by another trigger. A blocking match parks the
session in `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL`. A `NOTIFY_ONLY` match leaves the session
running on `researcher`, and the application raises the notice. The scaffold
supplies component names and routing decisions. The application must provide the
interface and connect human responses to the session.

## Correct arguments and handle timeouts

For `MODIFIED`, the application re-validates your corrected arguments against
the tool's parameter schema before it runs the corrected call. If validation
fails, it rejects the correction and does not execute it. Fix the reported
fields and submit a valid correction before the session can resume.

Each trigger sets `timeout_seconds`. The configuration's default timeout is
3600 seconds. The supplied financial trigger allows 3600 seconds and the
deletion challenge allows 900 seconds.

A `TIMEOUT` resolution emits `HITL_TIMEOUT` and ends the session as
`TASK_ABORTED`. It never approves the call. `DENIED` emits `HUMAN_REJECTED` and
also aborts the session. A notice alone requires no response and cannot time out
an approval wait of its own.

## Read the session status

| State | What it means for you |
| --- | --- |
| `RUNNING` | The agent is working on the task. |
| `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` | The supervisor has a matched trigger to handle. A blocking request waits for your response. |
| `RESUMING_LOOP` | The application accepted approval or valid corrections and is preparing to continue. |
| `TASK_ABORTED` | The task ended without completion. This is a terminal state. |
| `TASK_COMPLETED` | The task finished. This is a terminal state. |

The state machine accepts these transitions. Any other state and event pair
raises an error. Terminal states have no outgoing transitions.

| From | Event | To |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `RUNNING` | `HITL_TRIGGERED` | `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` |
| `RUNNING` | `TASK_DONE` | `TASK_COMPLETED` |
| `RUNNING` | `TASK_FAILED` | `TASK_ABORTED` |
| `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` | `HUMAN_APPROVED` | `RESUMING_LOOP` |
| `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` | `HUMAN_MODIFIED` | `RESUMING_LOOP` |
| `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` | `HUMAN_REJECTED` | `TASK_ABORTED` |
| `PENDING_HUMAN_APPROVAL` | `HITL_TIMEOUT` | `TASK_ABORTED` |
| `RESUMING_LOOP` | `LOOP_RESUMED` | `RUNNING` |
| `RESUMING_LOOP` | `TASK_DONE` | `TASK_COMPLETED` |
| `RESUMING_LOOP` | `TASK_FAILED` | `TASK_ABORTED` |

`APPROVED` maps to `HUMAN_APPROVED`. A valid `MODIFIED` correction maps to
`HUMAN_MODIFIED`. A notice emits neither event to release a pending request.

## Find the audit trail

The database table `hitl_records` holds one row per request. Use `session_id`
to find requests for a session and `hitl_id` to identify one request.
Each row records `trigger_id`, `action_required`, `proposed_tool_name`,
`proposed_arguments`, any `human_corrections`, and `resolution_status`.
`requested_at` and `resolved_at` show when the request opened and ended.
Compare the proposed arguments with the corrections to see what the human
changed. A notice row has `action_required` set to `NOTIFY_ONLY` and
`resolution_status` set to `PENDING`; it is not an outstanding approval. An
outstanding approval has `action_required` set to `APPROVE_REJECT_MODIFY` or
`MFA_CHALLENGE`. Read the session's current status in `agent_sessions`.

## Add a trigger

Edit `config/hitl_triggers.json`. Give each trigger a unique `trigger_id`, a
category, a description, a `match`, an `action_required`, a
`frontend_component`, and `timeout_seconds`. Connect the named component to the
application before you use a blocking trigger.

Choose the match kind that fits the operation. These worked examples show the
`match` object to put inside a trigger and the request that matches it.

| Match kind | Example `match` object | Matching request |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `argument_threshold` | `{"kind":"argument_threshold","field":"amount","op":"gt","value":500.0}` | Arguments `{"amount":600}` match. `{"amount":500}` does not. Use `APPROVE_REJECT_MODIFY` to review the amount. |
| `tool_name_in` | `{"kind":"tool_name_in","tool_names":["delete_record","purge_user"]}` | A proposed tool named `delete_record` matches. Use `MFA_CHALLENGE` to require authentication. |
| `argument_matches` | `{"kind":"argument_matches","field":"environment","pattern":"^production$"}` | Arguments `{"environment":"production"}` match. `{"environment":"staging"}` does not. Use `APPROVE_REJECT_MODIFY` to review production operations. |

`argument_threshold` compares the named argument numerically. Its operators
are `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, and `eq`. `tool_name_in` checks the proposed tool
name against the list. `argument_matches` applies the pattern to the string
form of the named argument. The router returns every matching trigger, so test
a matching request and a nonmatching request for each rule you add.

## Multiple blocking matches

A single operation can match more than one blocking trigger. A `delete_record`
call with `amount: 600` matches both the default financial trigger and the
default deletion trigger, so two blocking requirements exist.

The application creates one HITL record per blocking match. The session parks
on the first record it created. When the human resolves that record, the
application checks the remaining records for the same operation. If any is
still pending, the session parks on the next one. The operation runs only when
no blocking record for it remains pending.

The resolution endpoints resume the session only when no other blocking record
for that operation is pending. The update that moves a record from pending to
resolved is atomic. Two concurrent requests must not both consume the same
approval.

## Router precondition

`route()` refuses a session that is not `RUNNING`. Before the application
routes resumed work, it applies `LOOP_RESUMED` to move the session back to
`RUNNING`.

## Caller authorization

The caller must be authorized for the session before a resolution is accepted.
An authenticated caller with no access to the named session must be refused.
Record and session consistency (the hitl record belonging to the session in the
path) does not establish this. Check it separately.
